---- QUESTIONS: BOOTLOADER ---- 
>> B5: How does the bootloader read disk sectors? In particular, what BIOS interrupt
is used?
			When the bootloader runs, it uses the BIOS disk interrupt int 0x13 to pull data off the disk. This is achieved using function 0x42, which is the "extended read sectors" version. That means instead of passing the disk info directly in registers, it passes a pointer to a small structure in memory (the Disk Address Packet) that has the sector count, where to put the data, and the starting disk location.
			
>> B6: How does the bootloader decides whether it successfully finds the Pintos kernel?
			The bootloader decides whether it successfully finds the Pintos kernel by checking the result of the BIOS call. After running int 0x13, the carry flags in eflags was 0 (from 0x46), which confirms the lack of errors. Furthermore, after inspecting the register through p/x $ax, the output: 0x4200 affirms the function number of the extended read sectors mentioned previously. 

>> B7: What happens when the bootloader could not find the Pintos kernel?
			If the bootloader cannot find the Pintos kernel it will print an error like "Bad read" or "Not found" using BIOS video interrupts. Then it will just implement an infinite loop so nothing else runs.

>> B8: At what point and how exactly does the bootloader transfer control to the Pintos kernel?
			The bootloader transfers control to the Pintos kernel's entry point after it finishes loading and validating the image. In my attempt, I set a breakpoint at start (in start.S) to have the execution stop there. The transfer happens though a direct jmp to the kernel's entry address. 	

